<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-eigenvalues" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eigenvalue-motivation">
    <title>Motivation: Powers of a Matrix</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-power-motivation">
      <title>Can We Compute <m>A^{1000}</m> Efficiently?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Can we efficiently calculate <m>A^{1000}</m> if
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Consider the matrix
          <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and note that
          <m>P^{-1} = \frac{1}{7} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
          Let us compute <m>P^{-1}AP</m>:
          <md>
            P^{-1}AP
            = \frac{1}{7} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{1}{7} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 5 \\ 2 \amp -15 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -5 \end{bmatrix} = D,
          </md>
          a diagonal matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          How can the above calculation help us solve the original question? Rearranging,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              P^{-1}AP \amp = D
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              P \left( P^{-1}AP \right) P^{-1} \amp = PDP^{-1}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \left( PP^{-1} \right) A \left( PP^{-1} \right) \amp = PDP^{-1}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              A \amp = PDP^{-1}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          And therefore the inner factors telescope:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              A^{1000}
              \amp = \left( PDP^{-1} \right)\left( PDP^{-1} \right) \cdots \left( PDP^{-1} \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = PD \left( P^{-1}P \right) D \left( P^{-1}P \right) \cdots \left( P^{-1}P \right) DP^{-1}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = P D^{1000} P^{-1}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Powers of a diagonal matrix are trivial:
          <md>
            D^{1000} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -5 \end{bmatrix}^{1000}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2^{1000} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp (-5)^{1000} \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so
          <md>
            <mrow>
              A^{1000}
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix} 2^{1000} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp (-5)^{1000} \end{bmatrix}
                \frac{1}{7} \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \frac{1}{7} \begin{bmatrix}
                3(2)^{1001} + 5^{1000} \amp 2^{1001} - 2(5)^{1000} \\
                3(2)^{1000} - 3(5)^{1000} \amp 2^{1000} + 6(5)^{1000}
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-powers-of-diagonalizable">
      <title>Powers of a Diagonalizable Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is an <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>P</m> is an invertible
          <m>n \times n</m> matrix such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m>, then
          <m>A^k = PD^kP^{-1}</m> for each <m>k \in \mathbb{N}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <definition xml:id="def-diagonalizable">
      <title>Diagonalizing a Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The process of finding an <em>invertible</em> matrix <m>P</m> and a
          <em>diagonal</em> matrix <m>D</m> such that <m>A = PDP^{-1}</m> is known as
          <term>diagonalizing</term> the matrix <m>A</m>, and <m>P</m> is referred to as
          the <term>diagonalizing</term> matrix.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-diagonalization-questions">
      <title>Questions</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Are all matrices diagonalizable? If not, when is a matrix diagonalizable?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              How can we diagonalize a matrix <m>A</m>? (How can we find <m>P</m> and
              <m>D</m>?)
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-eigenvalue-definition">
    <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-eigenvalue">
      <title>Eigenvalue and Eigenvector</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be an <m>n \times n</m> matrix, <m>\lambda</m> a real number, and
          <m>\vec{x} \neq \vec{0} \in \R^n</m>. If <m>A\vec{x} = \lambda\vec{x}</m>, then
          <m>\lambda</m> is an <term>eigenvalue</term> of <m>A</m>, and <m>\vec{x}</m> is
          an <term>eigenvector</term> of <m>A</m> corresponding to <m>\lambda</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-eigenvector-check">
      <title>Verifying an Eigenvector</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
        <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Then
        <md>
          A\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 5 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
          = 5 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = 5\vec{x}.
        </md>
        This means <m>5</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> and
        <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>
        corresponding to eigenvalue <m>5</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-eigenvector-reflection">
      <title>A Geometric Eigenvector</title>
      <p>
        Consider reflection with respect to the <m>x</m>-axis in <m>\R^2</m>, with matrix
        <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and take
        <m>\vec{x} = \vec{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Then
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
          = (1) \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
        </md>
        so <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> with
        eigenvalue <m>\lambda = 1</m>. Geometrically this is obvious: points on the
        <m>x</m>-axis do not move under this reflection.
      </p>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-eigenvector-direction">
      <caption>
        Being an eigenvector is about direction. For
        <m>A = \left[\begin{smallmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -4 \end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>
        from <xref ref="eg-power-motivation" text="type-global" />, the vector
        <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an eigenvector:
        <m>A\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} = 2\vec{v}</m> stays on the
        dashed line through <m>\vec{v}</m>. The vector
        <m>\vec{w} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is not:
        <m>A\vec{w} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}</m> leaves the line through
        <m>\vec{w}</m>.
      </caption>
      <image width="72%">
        <prefigure label="pf-eigenvector-direction" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(460,300)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-1.0,-1.7,5.0,2.9)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
              <line endpoints="((-0.9,-0.45),(4.8,2.4))" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(4,2))" stroke="#7d5bb5" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(2,1))" stroke="blue" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(3,-1))" stroke="#d55e00" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(1,1))" stroke="red" thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
              <label p="(1.7,1.05)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,4)" color="blue"><m>\vec{v}</m></label>
              <label p="(3.9,1.75)" alignment="se" offset="(4,-6)" color="#7d5bb5"><m>A\vec{v} = 2\vec{v}</m></label>
              <label p="(0.8,1.1)" alignment="n" offset="(0,6)" color="red"><m>\vec{w}</m></label>
              <label p="(3.0,-1.05)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-6)" color="#d55e00"><m>A\vec{w}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-characteristic-polynomial">
    <title>The Characteristic Polynomial</title>

    <p>
      <term>Question: How can we calculate the eigenvalues of a matrix?</term>
      Rearranging the defining equation,
      <md>
        <mrow>
          A\vec{x} \amp = \lambda\vec{x}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \lambda\vec{x} - A\vec{x} \amp = \vec{0}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \lambda I \vec{x} - A\vec{x} \amp = \vec{0}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \left( \lambda I - A \right) \vec{x} \amp = \vec{0}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This is a system of linear equations with coefficient matrix
      <m>\lambda I - A</m>, and if there exists a nontrivial solution <m>\vec{x}</m>, then
      <m>(\lambda I - A)</m> is not invertible and therefore
      <md>
        \det(\lambda I - A) = 0.
      </md>
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-characteristic-polynomial">
      <title>Characteristic Polynomial</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>characteristic polynomial</term> of an <m>n \times n</m> matrix
          <m>A</m> is defined to be <m>c_A(\lambda) = \det(\lambda I - A)</m>, where
          <m>I</m> is the identity matrix.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-characteristic-polynomial">
      <title>Finding Eigenvalues</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the eigenvalues of
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            \lambda I - A
            = \begin{bmatrix} \lambda \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp \lambda \end{bmatrix}
              - \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \lambda - 1 \amp -2 \\ -3 \amp \lambda + 4 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so
          <md>
            \det(\lambda I - A) = (\lambda - 1)(\lambda + 4) - 6 = 0
            \; \longrightarrow \;
            \lambda^2 + 3\lambda - 10 = 0
            \; \longrightarrow \;
            (\lambda + 5)(\lambda - 2) = 0,
          </md>
          giving <m>\lambda_1 = -5</m> and <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>. These are exactly the
          diagonal entries of the matrix <m>D</m> in
          <xref ref="eg-power-motivation" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-finding-eigenvectors">
      <title>Finding the Eigenvectors</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <term>Question: How can we calculate the eigenvectors of a matrix?</term>
          Continue the previous example and find the eigenvectors.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          For each eigenvalue we solve
          <m>(\lambda I - A)\vec{x} = \vec{0}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>For <m>\lambda_2 = 2</m>:</term>
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ -3 \amp 6 \amp 0 \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array} \right]
            \; \Longrightarrow \;
            \vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 2t \\ t \end{bmatrix} = t \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad t \in \R \text{ and } t \neq 0.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>For <m>\lambda_1 = -5</m>:</term>
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} -6 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ -3 \amp -1 \amp 0 \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c} 3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array} \right]
            \; \Longrightarrow \;
            \vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} -t \\ 3t \end{bmatrix} = t \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad t \in \R \text{ and } t \neq 0.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          So <m>\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}</m> are basic eigenvectors of <m>A</m>
          corresponding to eigenvalues <m>2</m> and <m>-5</m> respectively. Note that
          these are precisely the columns of the matrix <m>P</m> in
          <xref ref="eg-power-motivation" text="type-global" /> <mdash /> which answers
          the question of how <m>P</m> was found.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
